timeline.body:
I have mixed feelings about this approach. I can see what it is doing, but I would prefer we don't use thread local. It is not obvious from just reading.
Perhaps this can be solved without it (the threadlocal approach is basically taking a parameter off the stack)

I think the clue of the problem is in [StreamOutput:L1112](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/pull/93944/files#diff-0d24047955e824f5340c51cc91a095c8d3559ed404534c084afb7171e57340dbR1112)
we loose the information about the nestedLevel there.

How about we change that line to ex.writeTo2(this,nestedLevel+1)
and within ElasticsearchException we would pass that level when writing an exception
```
--- a/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/ElasticsearchException.java
+++ b/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/ElasticsearchException.java
@@ -265,9 +265,13 @@ public class ElasticsearchException extends RuntimeException implements ToXConte
 
     @Override
     public void writeTo(StreamOutput out) throws IOException {
+        writeTo2(out, 0);
+    }
+
+    public void writeTo2(StreamOutput out, int nestedLevel) throws IOException {
         out.writeOptionalString(this.getMessage());
         out.writeException(this.getCause());
-        writeStackTraces(this, out, StreamOutput::writeException);
+        writeStackTraces(this, out, (o,v) -> o.writeException(v,v,nestedLevel+1) );
         out.writeMapOfLists(headers, StreamOutput::writeString, StreamOutput::writeString);
```
```
--- a/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/common/io/stream/StreamOutput.java
+++ b/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/common/io/stream/StreamOutput.java
@@ -1003,7 +1003,7 @@ public abstract class StreamOutput extends OutputStream {
         writeException(throwable, throwable, 0);
     }
 
-    private void writeException(Throwable rootException, Throwable throwable, int nestedLevel) throws IOException {
+    public void writeException(Throwable rootException, Throwable throwable, int nestedLevel) throws IOException {

```
(ignore method names)
would this work? wdyt?

